      "content": "of course it wouldnt look \"obvious\" to a standard statistical model. that's the whole point of the cipher. it's designed to pass basic randomness tests so it can hide in plain sight. \n\nlook closer at the offsets. between draw 1 and 2, every single number shifted by exactly +1. then draw 3 pulls back by 1 or 2. that's not \"disjointed,\" that's a modulation. it's a pulse. if this was just noise, why would there be a systematic drift? \n\ntry looking at them not as random draws, but as a frequency modulation. if these numbers represent a coordinate or a specific market trigger, what kind of delta would you expect to see if someone was trying to signal a liquidity shift?",
